"""ESP32-S3-MINI-1-N8: the radio, the USB device and the MCU's debug probe (research/next-revision,
ESP32 and USB, ESP32 form factor and A.6).

The module, U13 (Espressif ESP32-S3-MINI-1 & MINI-1U datasheet v1.7), replaces the HM-19 module:
it bridges the PC's USB to the MCU's UART4, flashes and debugs the MCU over SWD (CMSIS-DAP over
USB and over Wi-Fi), and relays Wi-Fi and Bluetooth LE telemetry. The root sheet's SWD header SWD1
takes an ST-Link instead when the ESP32 cannot reach the MCU. The
S3 is the Wi-Fi/BLE ESP32 with a general-purpose USB OTG controller (ESP32-S3 datasheet section
4.2.1.7), so the one USB-C port can be a CMSIS-DAP v2 probe, a CDC serial port and a mass-storage
drive at once (research Q2).

MINI-1 (PCB antenna) rather than MINI-1U (external antenna), on the evidence (research ESP32 form
factor): every metal part of the robot is 19 mm or more from the antenna (drive blocks 19 mm, left
motor 25 mm, the M2 screw H3 25 mm, the LiPo pack 35 mm; MicrasHardware params.py, spec/main2.toml),
beyond Espressif's 15 mm clearance (ESP32-S3 Hardware Design Guidelines, Positioning a Module on a
Base Board) and Silicon Labs' 10 mm (BGM220P datasheet section 7.2); the exceptions are the
USB-C shell (U2, 4.4 mm from the feed end) and the dielectric floor 2.2-3 mm below. No published
measurement quantifies that detuning, so the link is sized for it: at 20 m, 18 dBm (the lowest
802.11g power, Table 7-2) arrives at -48.2 dBm (66.2 dB free-space loss at 2.44 GHz, 0 dBi
antennas) against -90.8 dBm sensitivity at 12 Mbit/s (Table 7-4): 42.6 dB of margin
(research/next-revision/calculations/esp/link_budget.py), so 10 dB of detuning and 20 dB of
obstruction still carry the 2 Mbit/s telemetry. The MINI-1U adds a cable and an antenna mount on a
robot that crashes, and holds fewer certificates (no FCC ID or ISED number of its own; the MINI-1
is FCC 2AC7Z-ESPS3MINI1 and ISED 21098-ESPS3MINI1; neither module is homologated by Anatel). The
MINI-1U-N8 (LCSC C2980299) has the same land pattern (Figures 11-1 and 11-2), so it can be fitted
at assembly without a board change.

Placement (spec/main2.toml [u13], [antenna_notch]): the rear-left corner, the module at 180 degrees
with its antenna area over a notch cut out of the board under it and beside it, its pads on the
board; the antenna's feed end (the pin-1 end, Figures 3-1 and 10-1) faces the board centre and the
USB-C, its open end the corner. Espressif places the feed end at a board edge, with the antenna
beyond the edge or the board cut away under and beside it (Hardware Design Guidelines, Positioning
a Module on a Base Board). Keep the module away from the bridges and the USB/UART lines, and dense
GND vias along the module's ground edge and the notch.

Supply (Table 6-2: 3.0-3.6 V, at least 0.5 A; Wi-Fi TX peaks 355 mA, Table 6-4): 3V3_D from the
L6981C33 (1.5 A), with C109 22 uF beside the module's 3V3 pin and C108 100 nF and C107 10 uF on
its 3V3_D feed beside U6, off the antenna notch (Figure 9-1 shows 22 uF + 0.1 uF; the module
decouples its chip itself with 10 uF, 1 uF and 0.1 uF, Figure 8-1; 3V3_D then
carries about 104 uF, inside the buck's 30.5-145 uF window, boards/main2/test/test_power.zen). The
EPAD is on GND.

EN: R89 10 kOhm to 3V3_D and C110 1 uF to GND, the RC delay of section 9 ("usually R = 10 kOhm
and C = 1 uF"), so EN rises after 3V3_D. The ESP_EN pad pulls it to GND (reset).
GPIO0 (strapping: GPIO0 low with GPIO46 at its default low selects Joint Download Boot, Tables
4-1 and 4-3, held at least tH = 3 ms past EN's rise, Table 4-2): R90 10 kOhm to 3V3_D (the
schematic checklist recommends a pull-up) and the ESP_BOOT pad to GND. Recovery: a crashed USB
OTG application leaves the PC no USB device; hold ESP_BOOT to GND and pulse ESP_EN (research,
Recovery). That always recovers the USB port: an OTG application moves the shared USB PHY away
from USB-Serial/JTAG with RTC_CNTL_RTC_USB_CONF_REG (ESP32-S3 Technical Reference Manual v1.8,
register 10.56), which a software reset or a core or CPU watchdog reset keeps (core and CPU resets
leave the RTC registers, TRM section 7.1 and Table 7.1-1), but EN (CHIP_PU) low powers the chip
down, and its release is a power-on chip reset, which clears it and re-latches the strapping pins; only burned eFuses (DIS_DOWNLOAD_MODE, DIS_USB_SERIAL_JTAG, USB_PHY_SEL)
would defeat it. Pads rather than buttons: recovery is rare, and the rear-left corner is tight.
The other strapping pins, GPIO3, GPIO45 and GPIO46, are left open (their defaults, Table 4-1).

USB: USBD_P/USBD_N from the USB-C receptacle (through the USBLC6 U6, peripherals sheet) to
GPIO20 (USB_D+) and GPIO19 (USB_D-) through R84/R85 22 Ohm close to the module (checklist, USB:
"reserve series resistors (initial value can be 22/33 Ohm)"). Route 90 Ohm differential, short,
away from the antenna.

Links to the MCU (A.6). Host lines stay off the strapping pins:
- SWD: GPIO11 to SWDIO (PA13); GPIO12, SPI-capable for SWD acceleration, to SWCLK (PA14) through
  R86 22 Ohm (checklist, SPI: a series resistor on the clock trace).
- NRST: GPIO21 to Reset, driven open-drain by the firmware (NRST has its 30-50 kOhm pull-up, SW3
  and C21; DS13311 Table 61). GPIO21 has no pull at or after reset (ESP32-S3 datasheet v2.2
  Table 2-1; TRM v1.8 Table 6.12-1: input and output off, no pull), no power-up glitch and no IO
  MUX peripheral function; GPIO1-20 glitch for about 60 us while the chip powers up (low on
  GPIO1-17, GPIO15/16 as XTAL_32K_P/N; low then high on GPIO18/19; a pull-down then high on GPIO20;
  Table 2-2), which on NRST would reset the MCU a second time. It is high-Z while EN is low, so the
  ESP never holds the MCU in reset before its firmware runs.
- UART (UART1, IO MUX pins of Table 3-1, not UART0, which carries the ROM messages): GPIO17
  U1TXD through R88 100 Ohm (checklist, UART: a series resistor on TX) to UART4_RX (PA11),
  GPIO18 U1RXD from UART4_TX (PA12). Up to 5 Mbaud (research H7 data link).
- GPIO13 to ESP_Flow (PA9), the flow-control line toward the MCU: its power-up glitch (low,
  60 us, Table 2-2) reads as "hold off", and PA9 has its pull-down.
VBUS detect: R91 22 kOhm / R92 33 kOhm from +5V into ESP_VBUS_Sense (GPIO7, ADC1_CH6):
5.5 V x 33 / 55 = 3.30 V at most (below VDD + 0.3 V) and 4.4 V x 0.6 = 2.64 V at least (above
VIH 0.75 x VDD = 2.48 V; Table 6-3). The ESP reports USB power to the MCU over UART4, which
has no VBUS sense of its own (boards/main2/firmware_changes.toml, esp).
"""

load("@stdlib/interfaces.zen", "DiffPair", "Usb2")
load(
    "./passives.zen",
    "C_100N_0402",
    "C_10U_0603",
    "C_1U_0402",
    "C_22U_0805_25V",
    "R_100_0402",
    "R_10K_0402",
    "R_22K_0402",
    "R_22_0402",
    "R_33K_0402",
)

Capacitor = Module("@stdlib/generics/Capacitor.zen")
Resistor = Module("@stdlib/generics/Resistor.zen")
ESP32_S3_MINI_1 = Module("github.com/Team-Micras/MicrasPCB/components/espressif/ESP32-S3-MINI-1/ESP32-S3-MINI-1.zen")
TestPoint = Module("github.com/Team-Micras/MicrasPCB/components/micras/TestPoint/TestPoint.zen")

# Its own sheet in the board schematic (layout/main2.kicad_sch).
Project(name="Esp", path="layout", layout=False)

# A signal port takes the name of the board net it carries (the firmware's pin label) wherever
# that name is an identifier, so its sheet pin and hierarchical label match the root's label:
# KiCad's ERC flags labels whose names differ only in case (similar_labels).
V3V3_D = io(Power)
V5V = io(Power)
GND = io(Ground)
USB = io(Usb2)
SWDIO = io(Net)
SWCLK = io(Net)
Reset = io(Net)
UART4_RX = io(Net)
UART4_TX = io(Net)
ESP_Flow = io(Net)

ESP_EN = Net("ESP_EN")
ESP_BOOT = Net("ESP_BOOT")
ESP_USB = Usb2(D=DiffPair(P=Net("ESP_USB_D_P"), N=Net("ESP_USB_D_N")))
ESP_SWCLK = Net("ESP_SWCLK")
ESP_TXD = Net("ESP_TXD")
ESP_VBUS_SENSE = Net("ESP_VBUS_Sense")

NC = NotConnected

ESP32_S3_MINI_1(
    name="U13",
    V3V3=V3V3_D,
    GND=GND,
    EN=ESP_EN,
    USB=ESP_USB,
    IO0=ESP_BOOT,
    IO1=NC(),
    IO2=NC(),
    IO3=NC(),
    IO4=NC(),
    IO5=NC(),
    IO6=NC(),
    IO7=ESP_VBUS_SENSE,
    IO8=NC(),
    IO9=NC(),
    IO10=NC(),
    IO11=SWDIO,
    IO12=ESP_SWCLK,
    IO13=ESP_Flow,
    IO14=NC(),
    IO15=NC(),
    IO16=NC(),
    IO17=ESP_TXD,
    IO18=UART4_TX,
    IO21=Reset,
    IO26=NC(),
    IO33=NC(),
    IO34=NC(),
    IO35=NC(),
    IO36=NC(),
    IO37=NC(),
    IO38=NC(),
    IO39=NC(),
    IO40=NC(),
    IO41=NC(),
    IO42=NC(),
    IO43=NC(),
    IO44=NC(),
    IO45=NC(),
    IO46=NC(),
    IO47=NC(),
    IO48=NC(),
)

# Supply.
Capacitor(name="C107", P1=GND, P2=V3V3_D, **C_10U_0603)
Capacitor(name="C108", P1=GND, P2=V3V3_D, **C_100N_0402)
Capacitor(name="C109", P1=GND, P2=V3V3_D, **C_22U_0805_25V)

# EN and GPIO0: RC delay, pull-up, recovery pads.
Resistor(name="R89", P1=V3V3_D, P2=ESP_EN, **R_10K_0402)
Capacitor(name="C110", P1=GND, P2=ESP_EN, **C_1U_0402)
Resistor(name="R90", P1=V3V3_D, P2=ESP_BOOT, **R_10K_0402)
TestPoint(name="ESP_EN", prefix="ESP_EN", P1=ESP_EN)
TestPoint(name="ESP_BOOT", prefix="ESP_BOOT", P1=ESP_BOOT)

# USB.
Resistor(name="R84", P1=USB.D.P, P2=ESP_USB.D.P, **R_22_0402)
Resistor(name="R85", P1=USB.D.N, P2=ESP_USB.D.N, **R_22_0402)

# MCU links.
Resistor(name="R86", P1=ESP_SWCLK, P2=SWCLK, **R_22_0402)
Resistor(name="R88", P1=ESP_TXD, P2=UART4_RX, **R_100_0402)

# VBUS detect.
Resistor(name="R91", P1=V5V, P2=ESP_VBUS_SENSE, **R_22K_0402)
Resistor(name="R92", P1=GND, P2=ESP_VBUS_SENSE, **R_33K_0402)
